============================================================
  SECURITY REPORT — Score: 72/100 [MEDIUM]
  The codebase has several notable security concerns: unauthenticated seed endpoint and a hard‑coded password expose the system to unauthorized data manipulation; missing length constraints on user‑supplied strings could lead to resource exhaustion; rate limiting is absent on auth endpoints, and CORS is overly permissive. Addressing these issues will raise the security posture significantly.

  Findings (6):
  [HIGH    ] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The `/seed` endpoint is publicly accessible and allows creation of users, tags, and posts without any authentication or authorization checks.
             Fix: Add `Depends(get_current_user)` (or a dedicated admin role check) to the `seed` endpoint and restrict it to admin users only. Consider removing the endpoint from production.
  [HIGH    ] HardcodedSecret in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             A static password (`Password123`) is hard‑coded for all seeded users, exposing a predictable credential that could be used to compromise those accounts.
             Fix: Replace the hard‑coded password with a randomly generated one per user and store it securely, or require the admin to set passwords via a protected endpoint. Do not commit static secrets to source.
  [MEDIUM  ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/tag.py
             Tag name field has no `max_length` constraint, allowing arbitrarily long strings which can lead to DoS or storage exhaustion.
             Fix: Add `max_length=50` (or an appropriate limit) to the `name` field: `name: str = Field(..., min_length=1, max_length=50)`.
  [MEDIUM  ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/post.py
             Post title and content fields lack `max_length` limits, opening the API to excessively large payloads.
             Fix: Define reasonable maximum lengths, e.g., `title: str = Field(..., min_length=1, max_length=255)` and `content_markdown: str = Field(..., min_length=1, max_length=5000)`.
  [MEDIUM  ] RateLimitingAbsent in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             Login and registration endpoints have no rate‑limiting, making them vulnerable to credential‑stuffing and brute‑force attacks.
             Fix: Integrate a rate‑limiting solution (e.g., `slowapi` or a reverse‑proxy limit) on `/auth/login` and `/auth/signup` endpoints.
  [LOW     ] CORSIssue in app/main.py
             CORS is configured to allow any origin, which is overly permissive for a production environment.
             Fix: Restrict `allow_origins` to the known frontend domains, e.g., `allow_origins=["https://myapp.com"]`.

  Passed: No raw SQL queries – ORM used everywhere, Passwords are hashed using a secure function (`get_password_hash`), Endpoints that modify data require authentication
