  SECURITY REPORT — Score: 70/100 [MEDIUM]
  The codebase has several medium‑severity issues, notably an unauthenticated seed endpoint, overly permissive CORS settings, and missing input length validation. A high‑severity authentication gap and hard‑coded password hash pose immediate risks. Addressing these findings and adding rate limiting will substantially improve the security posture.

  Findings (8):
  [HIGH    ] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The `/seed` endpoint is publicly accessible and does not require any authentication, allowing anyone to create users, tags, and posts.
             Fix: Add `Depends(get_current_user)` (or a dedicated admin permission check) to the `seed` endpoint and restrict it to privileged users only.
  [MEDIUM  ] CORSIssue in app/main.py
             CORS is configured to allow any origin (`"*"`), which is unsafe for a production environment and can expose the API to CSRF attacks.
             Fix: Replace the wildcard with an explicit list of trusted origins, e.g., `allow_origins=["https://myfrontend.com"]`.
  [MEDIUM  ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/post.py
             String fields such as `title`, `content_markdown`, `cover_image_url`, and `status` have no maximum length constraints, allowing excessively large payloads that could lead to DoS or storage exhaustion.
             Fix: Add `max_length` to all string fields, e.g., `title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=255)`.
  [MEDIUM  ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/tag.py
             Tag name field lacks a `max_length` constraint, permitting arbitrarily long strings.
             Fix: Define a reasonable maximum, e.g., `name: str = Field(..., max_length=50)`.
  [MEDIUM  ] HardcodedSecret in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The seed route creates users with a hard‑coded password hash value (`"hashed_password"`). If left unchanged in production, all seeded accounts share the same password.
             Fix: Replace the placeholder with a securely generated hash (e.g., using `get_password_hash(os.getenv('SEED_USER_PASSWORD'))`) or remove the seed route from production deployments.
  [MEDIUM  ] MassAssignment in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             The helper `_make_user` automatically copies any non‑null column from the request into the `User` model, which can be abused to set privileged fields (e.g., `role`, `is_active`) if they exist in the database.
             Fix: Explicitly whitelist only the fields that should be set by the registration endpoint (e.g., `email`, `username`, `password_hash`) and ignore any others.
  [LOW     ] RateLimitingAbsent in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             Login and registration endpoints have no rate‑limiting, making them vulnerable to credential‑stuffing and brute‑force attacks.
             Fix: Integrate a rate‑limiting middleware (e.g., `slowapi` or `fastapi-limiter`) and apply limits such as 5 attempts per minute per IP.
  [LOW     ] WeakJWT in app/utils/auth.py (not shown)
             The JWT creation function is not visible, but the project does not enforce token expiration or use a strong secret key in the provided code.
             Fix: Ensure `create_access_token` includes an `exp` claim (e.g., 15‑30 minutes) and that the secret key is loaded from a secure environment variable.

  Passed: No raw SQL queries with string interpolation found, Passwords are hashed using a secure algorithm (bcrypt) via get_password_hash
